This test is described as reproducing #18906, but `RateLimiter` is a mock that accepts every call (`:328-332`), so it pins the routing and never runs `time_backoff`. With the real limiter, the `tools/list` call at `:351` would be the request that gets 429. `RateLimiterFactoryTest.php:80-104` shows how to build a real `RateLimiterFactory` over `InMemoryStorage` and `MockClock`:

~~~php
$factory = new RateLimiterFactory(
    [
        'enabled' => true,
        'id' => RateLimiter::MCP_ADMIN_API,
        'policy' => 'time_backoff',
        'reset' => '1 hours',
        'limits' => [
            ['limit' => 300, 'interval' => '60 seconds'],
            ['limit' => 1000, 'interval' => '10 minutes'],
        ],
    ],
    new InMemoryStorage(),
    static::createStub(SystemConfigService::class),
    $clock = new MockClock(),
);

$rateLimiter = new RateLimiter();
$rateLimiter->registerLimiterFactory(RateLimiter::MCP_ADMIN_API, $factory);
$mcpRateLimiter = new McpRateLimiter($rateLimiter);

for ($i = 0; $i < 300; ++$i) {
    $mcpRateLimiter->enforceForAdminApi($tokenRequest('tools/list', $i));
}
$clock->sleep(60);

$mcpRateLimiter->enforceForAdminApi($tokenRequest('initialize', 1));
$mcpRateLimiter->enforceForAdminApi($tokenRequest('notifications/initialized'));
$mcpRateLimiter->enforceForAdminApi($tokenRequest('tools/list', 2));
~~~

Please replace the test with one like this. Its outcome for the last line also settles the `tools/list` question from the other comment.

