this entire test is questionable, no assertions should be avoided as much as possible

Count the accepted requests instead, with a minimal inline config. It fails on exactly what `time_backoff` broke (one request accepted after the pause, then throttled), and it makes `testAClientOverItsLimitIsThrottled` redundant:

```php
#[DataProvider('endpointProvider')]
public function testTheFullLimitIsAvailableAgainAfterThePause(string $route, \Closure $enforce, \Closure $request): void
{
    $limiter = $this->limiter($route, ['policy' => 'sliding_window', 'limit' => 2, 'interval' => '1 minute', 'enabled' => true]);

    static::assertSame(2, $this->acceptedUntilThrottled($limiter, $enforce, $request));

    // a sliding window is empty again after two intervals without requests
    $this->sleep(120);

    static::assertSame(2, $this->acceptedUntilThrottled($limiter, $enforce, $request));
}

private function acceptedUntilThrottled(McpRateLimiter $limiter, \Closure $enforce, \Closure $request): int
{
    $accepted = 0;
    try {
        while ($accepted < 10) {
            $enforce($limiter, $request());
            ++$accepted;
        }
    } catch (McpException) {
    }

    return $accepted;
}
```

